SAM_BODY = f'''<div class="article-layout"><aside class="article-aside"><p class="kicker">The music file</p><ol><li><a href="#answer">What Hazel Eyes means</a></li><li><a href="#confirmed">What is confirmed</a></li><li><a href="#visuals">Reading the visuals</a></li><li><a href="#new-york">The New York record</a></li><li><a href="#tracklist">The tracklist</a></li><li><a href="#listen">What to listen for</a></li></ol><button class="copy-link" data-copy-link type="button">Copy link</button></aside><div class="article-body">
<p class="article-opening"><em>Hazel Eyes</em> appears to mean looking at love without the theatrical armour that once defined Sam Smith&rsquo;s public image. The fifth studio album, due 21 August 2026, is officially described as a romantic, personal ode to New York built around vulnerability, artistic freedom and self-reflection. Its first visual language is close, sepia-toned and bodily: eyes, skin, tattoos, hands and lovers occupying almost all the frame. The era is not rejecting glamour. It is moving the spectacle inward.</p>
<p>This is a pre-release analysis, not a review. The album has not been released at the time of publication, so any claim about its full sound, narrative or quality would be invention. What can be read responsibly is the material already public: the twelve-song tracklist, Smith&rsquo;s own description, the singles, official videos and the visual choices surrounding the release.</p>
<div class="summary-box" id="answer"><p class="kicker">The concise answer</p><h2><em>Hazel Eyes</em> is a romance framed as a way of seeing</h2><p>The title places attention on a person rather than an abstract statement. Official copy calls the record an ode to New York and a document of emotional vulnerability. The artwork turns that promise into a visual method: intimacy is photographed close enough to feel unguarded, but carefully composed enough to remain performance. The tension between exposure and control is the era&rsquo;s most interesting idea.</p></div>
<section id="confirmed"><h2>First, what is actually confirmed</h2><p><em>Hazel Eyes</em> is Sam Smith&rsquo;s fifth studio album and will be released through Capitol Records UK on 21 August 2026. The standard edition contains twelve tracks. The official store describes it as a heartfelt ode to New York, the city Smith now calls home, and names emotional vulnerability, artistic freedom and self-reflection as its central themes. Longtime collaborator Simon Aldred worked on the project.</p><p>Smith has said the album was written over more than three years with a small group of close friends. In a statement published by Universal Music in June, they described the record as very personal and romantic, and said they challenged themselves as a producer while following the project from beginning to end. Those are useful facts because they shift the frame away from a quick stylistic pivot. This is being presented as a sustained body of work made within a trusted circle.</p><p>Three songs had already been identified in official album material before release: &ldquo;My Guy,&rdquo; &ldquo;To Be Free&rdquo; and &ldquo;Love Is a Stillness.&rdquo; Feist appears on &ldquo;Moondance,&rdquo; while the TwoCity Chorus is credited on &ldquo;Oh Mother.&rdquo; Anything beyond those details, including claims about samples, lyrical subjects or the identity of the person behind the title, should remain unconfirmed unless Smith chooses to explain it.</p><div class="article-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Confirmed facts and editorial readings for Sam Smith Hazel Eyes" tabindex="0"><table class="article-visual-table"><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Confirmed</th><th>Editorial reading</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Release</td><td>21 August 2026, twelve tracks</td><td>A compact album rather than an overextended streaming package</td></tr><tr><td>Setting</td><td>Officially described as an ode to New York</td><td>The city may function as emotional structure, not postcard scenery</td></tr><tr><td>Process</td><td>Written over three years with a close group</td><td>Trust and continuity replace event-sized reinvention</td></tr><tr><td>Visuals</td><td>Sepia, close portraiture and physical contact</td><td>Softness is controlled, not careless</td></tr><tr><td>Sound</td><td>Only released music can be assessed</td><td>The full album could connect restraint with theatrical vocals</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>
{picture(SAM_ROOT, "hazel-eyes-album-art", "Official Hazel Eyes CD artwork showing Sam Smith in an intimate sepia portrait")}
<section id="visuals"><h2>The visual era begins at touching distance</h2><p>The album artwork does not offer the clean, frontal authority of a conventional star portrait. Smith is horizontal, pressed into another body, with tattooed arms crossing the frame. The face is visible but not isolated. That distinction matters. Celebrity photography often treats intimacy as a prop placed around an individual. Here, the other person occupies enough space to interrupt the idea of a self-contained subject.</p><p>Sepia gives the image the feeling of an object already handled and kept. It refers loosely to old photographs without imitating a specific period. The palette also removes the easy symbolism of colour. There is no obvious red for passion, blue for sadness or neon for nightlife. Skin, paper and ink sit in a narrow tonal range, so expression and proximity do the work.</p><p>The official &ldquo;My Guy&rdquo; image continues the closeness. Smith rests against a man whose face is partly withheld. It is tender without pretending the camera is absent. Script lettering introduces the polish of a dedication or old film title. Nothing about the composition is raw in the literal sense; every softness has been lit and placed. That is why it works. The era is not claiming that performance has disappeared. It is asking whether performance can make room for sincerity.</p><h3>Why the title is more specific than it sounds</h3><p>&ldquo;Hazel eyes&rdquo; is a familiar romantic phrase, almost dangerously familiar. It could have slipped into generic prettiness. As an album title, however, it does something precise: it makes sight reciprocal. Eyes are what Smith sees and what sees Smith back. After years in which their styling, body and public self became constant subjects of outside scrutiny, naming a record after another person&rsquo;s gaze can feel like a redirection of attention.</p><p>The colour hazel is also unstable. It changes between green, gold and brown with light. That makes it a useful metaphor for a record being introduced through ambiguity rather than a hard new persona. The visuals feel warm, but the titles include absence, theft, stillness and holding on. Romance is present, though not necessarily uncomplicated.</p></section>
<section id="new-york"><h2>New York is not just the backdrop</h2><p>Calling the album an ode to New York risks suggesting familiar images: brownstones, yellow taxis, piano bars, rain on avenues. The stronger possibility is that the city provides a social form. New York allows privacy in public. People live close, observe one another intensely and still grant strangers room to invent themselves. For an artist whose life has been interpreted at enormous scale, that combination of visibility and anonymity has obvious creative value.</p><p>Smith&rsquo;s June performance at the Stonewall National Monument Visitor Center also places the era within a specific civic history. Universal Music&rsquo;s announcement described the event as part of New York Pride weekend and connected it to queer history, community and resilience. That setting should not be flattened into aesthetic mood. Stonewall is not vintage scenery. It makes the album&rsquo;s language of freedom and sanctuary political as well as personal.</p><p>The New York claim will ultimately need to be heard in the record itself. A city album does not require street names in every chorus. Place can emerge through who is in the room, how musicians interact, the scale of arrangements or the kind of nightlife a song imagines. The most convincing odes often capture a city&rsquo;s habits rather than list its landmarks.</p><h3>From the stage to the room</h3><p>Smith&rsquo;s recent public work has often embraced grand silhouettes, ceremonial staging and visible transformation. It would be simplistic to call <em>Hazel Eyes</em> a return to the &ldquo;real&rdquo; person beneath those choices. Theatricality was real too. A better reading is spatial: this era narrows the distance between performer and listener.</p><p>The &ldquo;To Be Free&rdquo; visual still uses a body reaching upward under dramatic light. The gesture is hardly small. Yet the monochrome setting removes much of the surrounding machinery. Voice and body remain theatrical, while the frame is less crowded. If the album sustains that balance, it could avoid the false choice between confession and spectacle.</p></section>
{picture(SAM_ROOT, "hazel-eyes-to-be-free", "Official image from Sam Smith's To Be Free video with the singer reaching upward in blue light")}
<section id="tracklist"><h2>Reading the tracklist without inventing the songs</h2><p>Titles are invitations, not evidence. Still, their sequence can reveal how an album wants to be approached. <em>Hazel Eyes</em> opens with &ldquo;Everlasting Love,&rdquo; a phrase of total commitment, then immediately moves to the title track. The order places the ideal first and the person second. Whether the record confirms or complicates that movement will only be clear after release.</p><ol><li><strong>Everlasting Love</strong></li><li><strong>Hazel Eyes</strong></li><li><strong>Moondance featuring Feist</strong></li><li><strong>My Guy</strong></li><li><strong>When He&rsquo;s Gone</strong></li><li><strong>Thief</strong></li><li><strong>Love Is a Stillness</strong></li><li><strong>Sugar Rush</strong></li><li><strong>Oh Mother featuring the TwoCity Chorus</strong></li><li><strong>Constant Companion</strong></li><li><strong>Hold On</strong></li><li><strong>To Be Free</strong></li></ol><p>The middle run is especially suggestive. &ldquo;My Guy&rdquo; is followed by &ldquo;When He&rsquo;s Gone&rdquo; and &ldquo;Thief,&rdquo; moving from possession to absence and violation in three titles. Later, &ldquo;Constant Companion&rdquo; and &ldquo;Hold On&rdquo; lead into &ldquo;To Be Free.&rdquo; Holding and freedom are placed beside one another rather than treated as simple opposites.</p><p>Feist is an intriguing collaborator because her work can make restraint feel rhythmically alive. The TwoCity Chorus credit suggests a different scale, one in which a personal address meets collective voices. Those are observations about credited participants, not promises of a particular sound. Production details and performances should be judged when the recording is public.</p></section>
<section id="listen"><h2>What to listen for when the album arrives</h2><p>Begin with the relationship between vocal scale and arrangement. Smith can fill a room with very little accompaniment, which makes restraint a meaningful production decision rather than the absence of one. Notice whether quiet tracks leave genuine air around the voice or use polish to simulate intimacy. On larger songs, listen for whether choirs and strings deepen the writing or simply announce importance.</p><p>Then follow the pronouns and places. Does New York appear as a real network of rooms, streets and people, or remain an idea in the press materials? Does the &ldquo;you&rdquo; remain one person across the album? A coherent romance can be told by changing speakers and time periods, but the shifts should be audible rather than supplied by fan theory.</p><p>Finally, pay attention to humour, appetite and ordinary pleasure. Public discussion of Smith often concentrates on pain, identity or controversy. The title &ldquo;Sugar Rush&rdquo; and the ease of &ldquo;My Guy&rdquo; suggest that delight may be part of this record&rsquo;s seriousness. Romance becomes more believable when it includes errands, jokes and changing moods, not only declarations delivered at full volume.</p><h3>What would make the era genuinely new</h3><p>A new haircut, palette or genre reference is not enough. The meaningful change would be authorship becoming more visible: production choices that feel internally connected, collaborators serving a shared world and visual work that develops rather than repeating one image. Smith&rsquo;s statement about taking on a producer role raises that expectation.</p><p>The strongest version of <em>Hazel Eyes</em> would not apologise for theatrical skill. It would use that skill with sharper judgement, knowing exactly when a song needs the chandelier and when it needs the lamp beside the bed. The released singles suggest that both rooms may exist on the record.</p></section>
<section class="article-faq"><h2><em>Hazel Eyes</em> questions</h2><h3>When is Sam Smith&rsquo;s <em>Hazel Eyes</em> released?</h3><p>The album is scheduled for 21 August 2026 through Capitol Records UK.</p><h3>How many songs are on <em>Hazel Eyes</em>?</h3><p>The official standard tracklist contains twelve songs.</p><h3>What does the <em>Hazel Eyes</em> album title mean?</h3><p>Smith has not supplied one definitive public explanation. Official material frames the album around romance, vulnerability, New York and self-reflection. The title can reasonably be read as attention fixed on a particular person, but identifying that person would be speculation.</p><h3>Who features on the album?</h3><p>Feist is credited on &ldquo;Moondance,&rdquo; and the TwoCity Chorus appears on &ldquo;Oh Mother.&rdquo;</p></section>
<section class="source-note"><h2>Sources, status and image credits</h2><p>Release date, themes and tracklist come from the <a href="https://store.samsmithworld.com/products/hazel-eyes-cd-signed-art-card">official Sam Smith store</a>. Smith&rsquo;s comments on the writing and production process, plus the Stonewall performance context, were checked against <a href="https://www.universalmusic.com.br/2026/06/24/sam-smith-anuncia-seu-quinto-album-de-estudio-hazel-eyes-que-chega-em-21-de-agosto-e-lanca-o-novo-single-my-guy/">Universal Music&rsquo;s 24 June announcement</a>. Images are official Capitol Records and Sam Smith release artwork or official video stills, used editorially with credit. This pre-release analysis will be reviewed after 21 August 2026.</p></section>
<section><h2>The softness is not an absence of design</h2><p><em>Hazel Eyes</em> is arriving in careful focus. Its bodies are close, its palette is narrow and its language is direct. None of that makes it unperformed. The promising idea is that Smith no longer needs performance to create distance. It can become the craft that allows a private feeling to hold up in public.</p></section><div class="article-end"><span>End</span><p>Published by by.foro Editorial on 16 August 2026. Scheduled for factual review after the album release. For corrections, contact <a href="mailto:hello@byforo.com">hello@byforo.com</a>.</p></div></div></div>'''


